## 1. The desk-check: does the constrained Gaussian determinant factor exactly?

**Yes, and it is now a theorem.**  `ConstrainedDeterminant.lean` works with the
bordered (Lagrange-multiplier) operator

    Â = [ Δ  Qᵀ ; Q  0 ]

whose Gaussian normalisation is the constrained one `∫ dB δ(QB) e^{−½⟨B,ΔB⟩}`.

* `borderedOperator_det` — `det Â = (−1)^{#constraints} · det Δ · det(QΔ⁻¹Qᵀ)`.
* `log_abs_borderedOperator_det` — hence
  `log|det Â| = log|det Δ| + log|det(QΔ⁻¹Qᵀ)|`, the sign being a
  background-independent constant.
* `borderedOperator_inv` — the inverse of `Â` has the Schur-projected
  constrained propagator `G = G̃ − G̃Qᵀ(QG̃Qᵀ)⁻¹QG̃` as its `(1,1)` block, with the
  reduced inverse `(QG̃Qᵀ)⁻¹` in the border.  So the propagator used throughout
  this development *is* the propagator of the constrained Gaussian.
* `borderedHessian_eq_betaTrace` — differentiating the constrained normalisation
  twice in the background gives exactly `½Tr(G V₁ G V₂) − ½Tr(G S)`.

## 2. The gauge/constraint subtraction appears by itself

`GhostSubtraction.lean`, unconditionally (only cyclicity of the trace):

    betaTrace G V₁ V₂ S = betaTrace G̃ V₁ V₂ S + betaTrace K⁻¹ (ghost jets),
    K = Q G̃ Qᵀ,
    ghost jets:  −W(V₁), −W(V₂),  −W(S) + W₂(V₁,V₂) + W₂(V₂,V₁),
    W(X) = Q G̃ X G̃ Qᵀ,  W₂(X,Y) = Q G̃ X G̃ Y G̃ Qᵀ.

(`betaTrace_constrained_split`, `constraintSector_eq_betaTrace`.)  The
constraint sector is the *same* bubble-minus-seagull functional in the reduced
block, i.e. the geometric avatar of the ghost determinant, and it is exactly the
mixed jet of `K(s,t) = QΔ(s,t)⁻¹Qᵀ` produced by the resolvent expansion.
`borderedHessian_sub_fullHessian` records the analytic corollary: constrained
Hessian minus unconstrained Hessian = constraint-sector functional.  No ghost
diagram is reconstructed by hand.

## 3. Normalising the jets modulo the Ward kernel, before multiplying

`WardKernelReduction.lean`: for any `G` with `GQᵀ = 0` and `QG = 0`,

    betaTrace G (V₁+QᵀA₁+B₁Q) (V₂+QᵀA₂+B₂Q) (S+QᵀA₃+B₃Q) = betaTrace G V₁ V₂ S,

so only the classes `[V] ∈ End(H)/({QᵀA}+{BQ})` enter, and a literal jet may be
reduced modulo the Ward kernel before any matrix product is formed
(`betaTrace_ward_invariant`, `betaTrace_ward_invariant_constrained`).

## 4. Sign of the integrand: the bubble cannot be the culprit

`ConstrainedPositivity.lean`:

* `constrainedPropagator_gram` — the Schur projection preserves the Gram form:
  `G̃ = BᵀB ⟹ G = CᵀC` with `C = (1 − BQᵀK⁻¹QBᵀ)B`, because
  `P = BQᵀK⁻¹QBᵀ` is an orthogonal projection.  Hence
  `constrainedPropagator_posSemidef`.
* `trace_bubble_nonneg` / `trace_bubble_pos` — for a symmetric variation,
  `Tr(GVGV) = Tr(MᵀM) ≥ 0`, and `> 0` whenever `GVG ≠ 0`.
* `betaTrace_nonneg_of_seagull_nonpos`, `betaTrace_pos_of_seagull_nonpos` —
  therefore the diagonal one-loop functional is (strictly) positive as soon as
  the seagull trace is nonpositive.  All the sign risk sits in the seagull term.
  `ConstrainedDeterminantChecks.betaTrace_neg_of_seagull` shows the seagull
  hypothesis cannot be dropped.

## 5. Matrix-valued symbols and the finite Brillouin sum

`BlockBrillouin.lean` and `CMP109SymbolChain.lean` carry the reduction to
symbols with Lorentz/colour indices:

* `betaTrace_blockBrillouin` — for Fourier-block-diagonal data,
  `½Tr(GV₁GV₂) − ½Tr(GS) = ∑_q betaTrace (g q) (v₁ q) (v₂ q) (s q)`, i.e. the
  Brillouin integrand `I(q)` is the same functional evaluated on the symbols;
* `constrainedPropagator_blockDiagonal` — the constrained propagator is computed
  momentum by momentum: `G(q) = g̃(q) − g̃(q)Q(q)ᵀ(Q(q)g̃(q)Q(q)ᵀ)⁻¹Q(q)g̃(q)`;
* `constrainedGaussianHessian_brillouin` — the whole chain: constrained Gaussian
  normalisation ⟶ one exact finite sum `∑_q I(q)`;
* `brillouinIntegrand_ward`, `brillouinIntegrand_nonneg_of_seagull_nonpos`,
  `brillouinSum_pos_criterion` — the A1a target reduced to *pointwise*
  conditions on the literal symbols: Gram covariance, symmetric variation,
  nonpositive seagull, and one momentum where the propagator does not annihilate
  the variation.  No global control over the Brillouin zone and no near/far
  split is needed for that criterion.

## 6. A closed-form constrained symbol

`TransverseSymbol.lean` computes the Schur formula exactly for a rank-one
(Landau-type) constraint:

    G̃ = a·1, Q = uᵀ  ⟹  G = a·(1 − uuᵀ/|u|²),   G u = 0,

and instantiates it at the project's lattice momentum `p̂_μ = 2 sin(π p_μ)`:
`G(p) = (1 − p̂p̂ᵀ/p̂²)/p̂²`, with
`wilsonOperator_mul_transversePropagator` proving
`K(p)·G(p) = 1 − p̂p̂ᵀ/p̂²` for the Wilson quadratic operator
`K_{μν} = δ_{μν}p̂² − p̂_μp̂_ν`: the closed-form symbol inverts `K` on the
constraint kernel, and the gauge zero mode of `K` is precisely the direction the
constraint removes.  These conventions are the project's own
(`LatticeMomentum`); nothing is presented as a transcription of an external
text.

## 7. The margin, and A1b from irrelevance

`A1Margin.lean`:

* `A1_margin_lower_bound` / `A1_margin_positive` — the boxed inequality
  `β_j ≥ b_Z − Rint − C_R B θ/(1−θ) = b_−`, uniformly in the shell index (hence
  in the number of scales, the volume and the cutoff), and `β_j > 0` when
  `b_− > 0`;
* `sharedExpansion_lower_bound` — *one* expansion
  `β_j(g) = β_* + ∑_{k<j} R_{j,k}(g_k−g_*) + Rem_j(g)` delivers both the
  constant term (A1a) and the historical derivatives (A1b), with
  `β_j(g) ≥ β_* − C_R B θ/(1−θ) − ε`;
* `responseDecay_of_irrelevance` — geometric decay *derived from RG
  irrelevance*: `|R_{j,k}| ≤ C L^{−ω(j−k)}` with `L>1, ω>0` gives
  `HasResponseDecay` with `θ = L^{−ω} < 1` (`irrelevantRatio_lt_one`), so the
  contraction is not postulated as a metric assumption;
* `A1_margin_nonvacuous` — the hypothesis package is jointly satisfiable.

